-- #1513 / #1306, third instance of the class: a named-field VARIANT of a
-- multi-constructor data type, written out of declaration order.  Surfaced by the
-- adversarial review of the fix, MEASURED both ways: `VA { a = 1, b = 2 }` printed
-- `1` for `getB` on base -- wrong -- and prints `2` on the branch.
--
-- It is a genuinely different declaration form from its two sibling fixtures, not a
-- respelling, and each difference is one a narrower fix could have missed:
--   * the constructor is one of SEVERAL, so the cell carries a real tag and
--     `cellTag`/`ctorOrdinal` are in play, not just a single-ctor record cell;
--   * the CONSTRUCTOR NAME IS NOT THE TYPE NAME (`VA` vs `V`), and the field-order
--     table is keyed by the CONSTRUCTOR while `getA : V -> Int` mentions the TYPE.
--
-- 🚨 `VB` USED TO BE `VB Int`, A POSITIONAL SIBLING WITH NO FIELD LABELS, AND THAT
-- MADE THIS PROGRAM ILL-TYPED -- it just had no way to say so until #1468's
-- positional-sibling gap was closed.  `getA v = v.a` projects a field off a value
-- statically typed `V`, and a `V` carries no static constructor tag, so the access
-- is only well-typed when EVERY constructor of `V` declares the field; the
-- positional `VB` declared neither `a` nor `b`.  The fixture's own purpose is
-- FIELD-ORDER INDEXING in a multi-constructor CELL layout, not field-owner
-- well-typedness -- it predates that fix and exercised the bad shape only by
-- accident -- so the repair is HERE, not in the checker.  ⚠️ Do NOT restore the
-- positional form to "make the gate green": that would be re-opening #1468.
--
-- `VB` therefore now declares `b`/`a` too, in the SAME slot order as `VA`.  The
-- order is deliberately not permuted: two constructors of one type declaring the
-- same label at DIFFERENT slots is #1465 (a dispatched tag-based selector), which
-- is not implemented, so permuting here would pin a wrong answer rather than test
-- one.  With both constructors declaring both labels the multi-constructor cell
-- with a real tag -- the property this fixture exists for -- is unchanged.
--
-- EXPECTED VALUE, HAND-DERIVED from this source before any engine was run --
-- pinned at test/engine_value_pins/engine/record_variant_field_order.pin:
--
--   (1, 2)
--
--   the literal sets `a = 1` and `b = 2` BY NAME; the declaration orders the slots
--   `b` then `a`, so a positional fill in WRITTEN order would put `1` in slot 0
--   (`b`) and `2` in slot 1 (`a`) and the pair would read `(2, 1)` -- the exact
--   transposition, which is why both components are pinned rather than one.
--   Unchanged by the `VB` repair: `main` still builds a `VA`, and `VB`'s slots are
--   ordered identically, so neither the layout nor the value it yields moved.

data V =
  | VA { b : Int, a : Int }
  | VB { b : Int, a : Int }

getA : V -> Int
getA v = v.a

getB : V -> Int
getB v = v.b

main =
  let v = VA { a = 1, b = 2 }
  (getA v, getB v)
